      "text": "1. We know that Timothy multiplies 1000 by a factor to get an integer quotient. Let's denote this factor as \\( f \\), so \\( 1000 \\times f \\) must be an integer. This implies that \\( f \\) must be a divisor of 1000.\n2. First, we need to determine the prime factorization of 1000:\n   \\[\n   1000 = 10^3 = (2 \\times 5)^3 = 2^3 \\times 5^3\n   \\]\n3. The divisors of 1000 are all numbers of the form \\( 2^a \\times 5^b \\), where \\( 0 \\leq a \\leq 3 \\) and \\( 0 \\leq b \\leq 3 \\). This gives us:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total number of divisors} = (3+1)(3+1) = 4 \\times 4 = 16\n   \\]\n4. The divisors of 1000 are:\n   \\[\n   1, 2, 4, 5, 8, 10, 20, 25, 40, 50, 100, 125, 200, 250, 500, 1000\n   \\]\n5. Thus, there are 16 possible divisors of 1000.\n6. Since Josef can pick any of these 16 divisors, there are 16 integers for which Timothy's quotient is an integer.\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{16}\n\\]",
