      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine how many integers Josef can pick such that when we divide 1000 by that integer, the quotient is an integer.\n\nAn integer \\( d \\) divides 1000 if and only if 1000 is divisible by \\( d \\). This means that \\( d \\) is a divisor of 1000. The first step is to find all the divisors of 1000.\n\nTo do this, we can perform the prime factorization of 1000:\n\\[\n1000 = 10^3 = (2 \\times 5)^3 = 2^3 \\times 5^3\n\\]\nThe number of divisors of a number \\( n = p_1^{e_1} \\times p_2^{e_2} \\times \\cdots \\times p_k^{e_k} \\) is given by:\n\\[\n(e_1 + 1)(e_2 + 1) \\cdots (e_k + 1)\n\\]\nFor \\( 1000 = 2^3 \\times 5^3 \\), the number of divisors is:\n\\[\n(3+1)(3+1) = 4 \\times 4 = 16\n\\]\nThus, 1000 has 16 divisors. These divisors are:\n\\[\n1, 2, 4, 5, 8, 10, 20, 25, 40, 50, 100, 125, 200, 250, 500, 1000\n\\]\n\nSince Josef can pick any of these 16 integers, the number of integers Josef could pick such that Timothy's quotient is an integer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{16}\n\\]",
